X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Nilesh Shinde
DTSTART:20261117T210000Z
DTEND:20261117T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20261103T094739Z
UID:20261117T210000Z-2471900@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261117T210000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/137/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,800,800/33265_shinde-nilesh.rev.1794617124.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) we
 lcomes Dr. Nilesh Shinde (O'Neill School of Public and Environmental Affai
 rs) for Land\, Policy\, and Documented Violence on Brazil's Amazon Frontie
 r\, part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.  Tuesday\, Nov
 ember 17th | 4-5PM | Hamilton Lugar School GA3067Abstract: Can rules gover
 ning land and forests make people safer on Brazil's Amazon frontier? This 
 talk brings together three studies of rural violence\, land registration\,
  and forest enforcement. Drawing on 24 years of records from Brazil's Past
 oral Land Commission\, I show that Indigenous people account for a growing
  share of documented murder victims and examine how the geography of recor
 ded violence has shifted. I then assess two policies: Pará's land registr
 ation mandate\, which was followed by fewer recorded threats and conflicts
 \, and Brazil's priority municipality program\, which substantially reduce
 d deforestation. The evidence for the program's social benefits is less ce
 rtain because estimates of violence depend on which municipalities are use
 d for comparison. Together\, the studies show why evaluating environmental
  policy requires careful attention to both the limits of violence records 
 and the reasons places were selected for intervention.Bio: Nilesh Shinde i
 s an Assistant Professor at Indiana University Bloomington's O'Neill Schoo
 l of Public and Environmental Affairs. An environmental economist and engi
 neer\, he studies how property rights\, institutions\, and environmental p
 olicy shape land and water use. His research spans deforestation in the Br
 azilian Amazon\, groundwater and irrigation in India\, and water pollution
  in the United States.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261111T204040Z
LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463745-clacs-faculty-research-sympo
 sium-with-nilesh
