BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 64": "https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2482233-the-paradox-of-precommitment-making-constitutions"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_64
SUMMARY:The Paradox of Precommitment: Making Constitutions in Deeply Divid
 ed Societies
DTSTART:20261119T170000Z
DTEND:20261119T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20261112T102312Z
UID:20261119T170000Z-2482233@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261119T170000Z
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:How can constitutions provide stability when the most conteste
 d political questions remain unresolved? Using Ethiopia's 1995 Constitutio
 n as a case study\, this talk explores the paradox of constitutional preco
 mmitment and the risks of entrenching disputed issues such as sovereignty
 \, self-determination\, and territorial authority. Drawing on constitution
 al theory\, history\, and institutional analysis\, it argues that durable 
 constitutional systems require both strong protections for democratic gove
 rnance and credible mechanisms for revisiting unsettled foundational quest
 ions.  About the speaker: Alemayehu Fentaw Weldemariam is a CCD PhD Fellow
  at the IU Maurer School of Law.&#160\;
GEO:39.165074;-86.526424
LAST-MODIFIED:20261112T152733Z
LOCATION:Henderson House
URL:https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2482233-the-paradox-of-precommitmen
 t-making-constitutions
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  How can constitutions provide stability 
 when the most contested political questions remain unresolved? Using Ethio
 pia's 1995 Constitution as a case study\, this talk explores the paradox o
 f constitutional precommitment and the risks of entrenching disputed issue
 s such as sovereignty\, self-determination\, and territorial authority. Dr
 awing on constitutional theory\, history\, and institutional analysis\, it
  argues that durable constitutional systems require both strong protection
 s for democratic governance and credible mechanisms for revisiting unsettl
 ed foundational questions.</p><p>  About the speaker: Alemayehu Fentaw Wel
 demariam is a CCD PhD Fellow at the IU Maurer School of Law.&#160\;</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>Center for Constitutional Democracy</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2482233
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:Center for Constitutional Democracy
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 64": "https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2377394-indiana-tax-court-oral-argument"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_64
SUMMARY:Indiana Tax Court Oral Argument
DTSTART:20261119T170000Z
DTEND:20261119T183000Z
DTSTAMP:20260811T101356Z
UID:20261119T170000Z-2377394@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261119T170000Z
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:The Indiana Tax Court will visit the IU Maurer School of Law t
 o hear oral argument in Northern Indiana Public Service Company\, LLC v. I
 ndiana Department of State Revenue\, Case No. 24T-TA-00017.   The Tax Cour
 t is a specialized appellate court with exclusive jurisdiction over appeal
 s from final determinations of the Indiana Department of State Revenue and
  the Indiana Board of Tax Review and certain appeals from the Department o
 f Local Government Finance. It hears disputes over state and local taxes\,
  including property\, income\, sales\, and excise taxes\, which touch the 
 finances of Hoosier individuals\, families\, and businesses alike.  Attend
 ees will have a rare chance to see this specialized court in action. Becau
 se the Tax Court's decisions are reviewed directly by the Indiana Supreme 
 Court\, rather than by the Court of Appeals\, the proceedings offer a dist
 inctive look at how Indiana's tax system is shaped by the judiciary.  A su
 mmary of the case can be found here (https://events.iu.edu/live/files/8523
 -nipsco-bench-memopdf). Following the argument\, attendees may participate
  in an informal Q&amp\;A session with the judge and attorneys. While food 
 is not permitted during the argument\, food is allowed during the Q&amp\;A
 .  Attendees are asked to observe courtroom etiquette:Remove hats before e
 ntering the courtroom.  Stand when judges enter and leave the courtroom.  
 Turn off or silence cell phones before entering the courtroom. Do not use 
 phones while court is in session.  No video\, audio recording\, or photogr
 aphy is allowed once court is in session.  No food or beverages are allowe
 d in the courtroom  Refrain from speaking during the proceedings
GEO:39.165074;-86.526424
LAST-MODIFIED:20261103T172759Z
LOCATION:LOWELL E. BAIER HALL/IU MAURER SCHOOL OF LAW
URL:https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2377394-indiana-tax-court-oral-argu
 ment
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The Indiana Tax Court will visit the IU 
 Maurer School of Law to hear oral argument in <em>Northern Indiana Public 
 Service Company\, LLC v. Indiana Department of State Revenue</em>\, Case N
 o. 24T-TA-00017. <em></em></p><p>  The Tax Court is a specialized appellat
 e court with exclusive jurisdiction over appeals from final determinations
  of the Indiana Department of State Revenue and the Indiana Board of Tax R
 eview and certain appeals from the Department of Local Government Finance.
  It hears disputes over state and local taxes\, including property\, incom
 e\, sales\, and excise taxes\, which touch the finances of Hoosier individ
 uals\, families\, and businesses alike.</p><p>  Attendees will have a rare
  chance to see this specialized court in action. Because the Tax Court's d
 ecisions are reviewed directly by the Indiana Supreme Court\, rather than 
 by the Court of Appeals\, the proceedings offer a distinctive look at how 
 Indiana's tax system is shaped by the judiciary.</p><p>  A <a class="file 
 file_8523 lw_files_pdf" title="NIPSCO _ Bench Memo.pdf" href="https://even
 ts.iu.edu/live/files/8523-nipsco-bench-memopdf" target="_blank" rel="noope
 ner">summary of the case can be found here</a>. Following the argument\, a
 ttendees may participate in an informal Q&amp\;A session with the judge an
 d attorneys. While food is not permitted during the argument\, food is all
 owed during the Q&amp\;A.</p><p>  Attendees are asked to observe courtroom
  etiquette:</p><ul><li>Remove hats before entering the courtroom.  </li><l
 i>Stand when judges enter and leave the courtroom.  </li><li>Turn off or s
 ilence cell phones before entering the courtroom. Do not use phones while 
 court is in session.  </li><li>No video\, audio recording\, or photography
  is allowed once court is in session.  </li><li>No food or beverages are a
 llowed in the courtroom  </li><li>Refrain from speaking during the proceed
 ings  </li></ul>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  <strong>Chelsey Willis Browning<br /></str
 ong>Assoc. Director of Events and Communications<br />\n  <a href="mailto:
 redmonds@iu.edu">cwbrowni@iu.edu</a><br />\n  <br />\n  <strong>Maurer Law
  Events</strong><br />\n  <a href="mailto:eventlaw@iu.edu">eventlaw@iu.edu
 </a>&#160\;<br />\n  <br />\n  <strong>Indiana Tax Court</strong><br />\n 
  115 W. Washington Street<br />\n  Suite 960S<br />\n  Indianapolis\, IN 4
 6204\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2377394
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
END:VEVENT
