DESCRIPTION:Lecture: October 27\, 6:30–8pmExhibition: October 27–Decem
 ber 6\, 2026\, Mon.–Thur. 10am–4pm when IU classes are in session  Lig
 htbound studies the production of space in urban environments\, weaving na
 rratives across photography\, print\, spatial interventions\, and sculptur
 e\, with a focus on materials and sites in transition.  Architecture becom
 es an entry-point to questions of land use\, ownership and access\, bodily
  experience and perception\, and timeframes ranging from the human to the 
 geological. Lightbound invites viewers to consider and participate in the 
 continual (re)construction of meaning in urban environments.  Frances Ligh
 tbound holds a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\, Scotland\, and an M.F.A. 
 from the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. Recent solo exhibitions i
 nclude Louise Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lubbock\, TX)\, RULE Gallery (M
 arfa\, TX)\, and the John David Mooney Foundation (Chicago\, IL)\; her wor
 k has been exhibited internationally at venues in London\, Glasgow and Ber
 lin. Lightbound is a Lecturer at School of the Art Institute of Chicago in
  the departments of Print media and Photography.The J. Irwin Miller Archit
 ecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Series brings prominent working arti
 sts and architects to Columbus. Lectures take place in the auditorium and 
 exhibitions in the gallery\, both located within The Republic Building\, 3
 33 Second Street in Columbus. The gallery is open Monday through Friday fr
 om 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All events are free and open to the public.
GEO:39.199929;-85.921713
LAST-MODIFIED:20260910T162141Z
LOCATION:The Republic Building
URL:https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2412144-frances-lightbound-the-la
 nd-dissolves-in-water-exhib
