X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463756-clacs-faculty-research-symposium-with-luciana"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Luciana Namorato
DTSTART:20261203T210000Z
DTEND:20261203T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20261103T094733Z
UID:20261203T210000Z-2471899@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261203T210000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/137/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,2013,2013/33266_Namorato1.rev.1794617124.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) we
 lcomes Dr. Luciana Namorato for The Other Fridas: Hidden Voices\, New Hist
 ories &amp\; Future Agendas of Latin American Women Artists\, part of the 
 Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.Thursday\, December 3rd\, 4-5PM
  at the Hamilton Lugar School (355 N Eagleson Ave.) GA1060  This presentat
 ion explores the recognition and representation of Latin American women ar
 tists\, considering how their voices and experiences can expand our unders
 tanding of the region's artistic histories.Bio:Luciana Namorato is Associa
 te Professor of Portuguese and Luso-Brazilian Literatures at Indiana Unive
 rsity Bloomington. She holds an M.A. and a Ph.D. from the University of No
 rth Carolina at Chapel Hill. Her research focuses on Luso-Brazilian and La
 tin American literatures\, with particular attention to the relationships 
 among literature\, gender\, and ethnic and national identities. As a Fulbr
 ight fellow\, she conducted postdoctoral research at the University of Coi
 mbra. She is co-editor of The Other Fridas: The Lives and Works of Latin A
 merican Women Artists (Lexington Books\, 2025)\, among other publications.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261111T175635Z
LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463756-clacs-faculty-research-sympo
 sium-with-luciana
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  The Center for La
 tin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) welcomes Dr. <strong>Luciana Na
 morato</strong> for <strong><em>The Other Fridas: Hidden Voices\, New Hist
 ories &amp\; Future Agendas of Latin American Women Artists</em></strong>
 \, part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.</p><p data-pm-s
 lice="1 1 []"><strong>Thursday\, December 3rd</strong>\, <strong>4-5PM</st
 rong> at the <strong>Hamilton Lugar School</strong> (355 N Eagleson Ave.) 
 <strong>GA1060</strong></p><p>  This presentation explores the recognition
  and representation of Latin American women artists\, considering how thei
 r voices and experiences can expand our understanding of the region's arti
 stic histories.</p><p><strong>Bio:</strong></p><p><strong>Luciana Namorato
 </strong> is Associate Professor of Portuguese and Luso-Brazilian Literatu
 res at Indiana University Bloomington. She holds an M.A. and a Ph.D. from 
 the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. Her research focuses on L
 uso-Brazilian and Latin American literatures\, with particular attention t
 o the relationships among literature\, gender\, and ethnic and national id
 entities. As a Fulbright fellow\, she conducted postdoctoral research at t
 he University of Coimbra. She is co-editor of The Other Fridas: The Lives 
 and Works of Latin American Women Artists (Lexington Books\, 2025)\, among
  other publications.</p><p><picture class="lw_image lw_image32516  lw_alig
 n_left"><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type="image/webp" srcset="https
