ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://browncounty.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/
 10/2-300x240-5.jpeg
CATEGORIES:Entertainment,Live Music
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261029
DESCRIPTION:Record-setting 10-time CMA Musician of the Year Mac McAnally w
 ill make a stop at the Brown County Music Center in Nashville\, Ind.\, on 
 Friday\, December 4\, 2026. Mac McAnally is a Nashville Songwriters Hall 
 of Fame\, Mississippi Musicians Hall of Fame and Alabama Music Hall of Fam
 e inductee. The renowned songwriter\, singer\, and instrumentalist has wri
 tten dozens of hit records for other artists\, several of which peaked at 
 No. 1. His own recording career began with debut hit single “It’s A Cr
 azy World” and has grown to 14 album releases. He has written hits for A
 labama\, Jimmy Buffett\, Kenny Chesney and Sawyer Brown\, and has studio c
 redits that include albums by Toby Keith\, Linda Ronstadt\, George Strait 
 and Dolly Parton among many others.\n
LAST-MODIFIED:20261029
LOCATION:200 Maple Leaf Boulevard\, Nashville\, IN 47448
ORGANIZER;CN="Brown County Music Center":MAILTO:boxoffice@browncountymusic
 center.com
PRIORITY:5
TRANSP:OPAQUE
URL:https://browncounty.com/events/mac-mcanally/
X-SOURCE:Brown County Events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2349572-rethinking-refugee-integration-evidence-from-newly"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Rethinking Refugee Integration: Evidence from Newly Resettled Afgh
 ans in the United States
DTSTART:20261204T200000Z
DTEND:20261204T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20260923T152440Z
UID:20261204T200000Z-2428199@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261204T200000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/491/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,612,792/31473_Mitra_presentation_graphic-1-8.5_x_11_Print
 _Flyer_Simple.rev.1783990567.png
DESCRIPTION:U.S. refugee policy continues to emphasize rapid employment an
 d economic self-sufficiency as primary indicators of successful integratio
 n. Drawing on a program of research with newly resettled Afghan households
  in Missouri and Oregon\, this presentation examines refugee integration t
 hrough a multidimensional lens that extends beyond employment and income. 
  Using survey data from Afghan households in St. Louis\, Missouri (N = 184
 ) and Portland\, Oregon (N = 150)\, alongside a national sample of refugee
  households from the Annual Survey of Refugees (N = 1\,386)\, we examine m
 ultidimensional deprivation and its determinants among refugees resettled 
 in the United States. Findings reveal widespread multidimensional deprivat
 ion\, affecting 62% of households in St. Louis\, 74% in Portland\, and 70%
  of the national refugee sample. Household size and English language profi
 ciency emerged as consistent predictors of deprivation across settings. At
  the same time\, local contexts mattered: lower housing costs and greater 
 economic security in St. Louis were associated with lower rates of multidi
 mensional deprivation compared with Portland.  To better understand how re
 fugees experience integration beyond material conditions\, we draw on a fo
 llow-up mixed-methods study of 203 Afghan households and five focus group 
 discussions. Quantitative findings indicate that stronger social connected
 ness within Afghan communities was associated with higher quality of life
 \, whereas integration with Americans was not. Qualitative findings reveal
 ed strong intra-ethnic social ties alongside limited bridging connections 
 to the broader host society. Participants described declining quality of l
 ife during the first five years of resettlement despite a strong sense of 
 community belonging.  Together\, these findings challenge employment-cente
 red approaches to refugee integration and highlight the importance of mult
 idimensional frameworks that account for material well-being\, social conn
 ectedness\, and quality of life.  All Colloquium events will be held in St
 udent Building Room 005 from 3:00 PM- 4:00 PM and on Zoom at: https://iu.z
 oom.us/j/81721952791?jst=1
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T192440Z
LOCATION:Geography Building
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2349572-rethinking-refugee-integrati
 on-evidence-from-newly
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">  U.S
 . refugee policy continues to emphasize rapid employment and economic self
 -sufficiency as primary indicators of successful integration. Drawing on a
  program of research with newly resettled Afghan households in Missouri an
 d Oregon\, this presentation examines refugee integration through a multid
 imensional lens that extends beyond employment and income.</div><div></div
 ><div>  Using survey data from Afghan households in St. Louis\, Missouri (
 N = 184) and Portland\, Oregon (N = 150)\, alongside a national sample of 
 refugee households from the Annual Survey of Refugees (N = 1\,386)\, we ex
 amine multidimensional deprivation and its determinants among refugees res
 ettled in the United States. Findings reveal widespread multidimensional d
 eprivation\, affecting 62% of households in St. Louis\, 74% in Portland\, 
 and 70% of the national refugee sample. Household size and English languag
 e proficiency emerged as consistent predictors of deprivation across setti
 ngs. At the same time\, local contexts mattered: lower housing costs and g
 reater economic security in St. Louis were associated with lower rates of 
 multidimensional deprivation compared with Portland.</div><div></div><div>
   To better understand how refugees experience integration beyond material
  conditions\, we draw on a follow-up mixed-methods study of 203 Afghan hou
 seholds and five focus group discussions. Quantitative findings indicate t
 hat stronger social connectedness within Afghan communities was associated
  with higher quality of life\, whereas integration with Americans was not.
  Qualitative findings revealed strong intra-ethnic social ties alongside l
 imited bridging connections to the broader host society. Participants desc
 ribed declining quality of life during the first five years of resettlemen
 t despite a strong sense of community belonging.</div><div></div><div>  To
 gether\, these findings challenge employment-centered approaches to refuge
 e integration and highlight the importance of multidimensional frameworks 
 that account for material well-being\, social connectedness\, and quality 
 of life.</div><div></div><div>  All Colloquium events will be held in Stud
 ent Building Room 005 from 3:00 PM- 4:00 PM and on Zoom at: https://iu.zoo
 m.us/j/81721952791?jst=1</div>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  refugees@iu.edu\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2428199
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/491/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,612\,792/31473_Mitra_presentation_graphic-1-
 8.5_x_11_Print_Flyer_Simple.rev.1783990567.png
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Flyer
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Rethinking Refugee Integration: Evidence from N
 ewly Resettled Afghans in the United States with Dr. Mitra Naseh\, Assista
 nt Professor at Washington University in St. Louis.&#160\;\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
