SUMMARY:Devon Walker from SNL Returns to Bloomington!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20270130T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20270130T210000
UID:20270130-devon-walker-from-snl-returns-to-bloomin-1900
DESCRIPTION:Devon can most recently be seen in LOL Network/Kevin Hart exec
 utive produced special that was released late this summer on Hulu. Previou
 sly\, Devon performed in the main cast of SATURDAY NIGHT LIVE in season 50
  after joining as a featured player in 2022. Devon has written on Phoebe R
 obinson’s series EVERYTHING’S TRASH and has also served on staff of th
 e hit Netflix animated series BIG MOUTH. Originally from Austin\, Devon 
 is a stand-up comic and writer currently living in Brooklyn\, recently her
 alded as a “young phenom” and “millennial wunderkind." Devon was s
 elected as a Comic to Watch for the “Up Next” show at the inaugural Co
 medy Central’s Clusterfest in 2017 and was named one of the "Funniest Pe
 ople in Austin" in both 2017 and 2018. He was also recently named in Vult
 ure's list of best new comedians for 2022. His dad is really proud of him.
  Devon Walker - SNL Reel https://video.wmeagency.com/re...
LOCATION:The Comedy Attic\, 123 S Walnut Street\, Bloomington\, IN
URL:https://www.comedyattic.com/shows/392392
X-SOURCE:The Comedy Attic
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 353": "https://events.iu.edu/iumaa/event/hidden-mythologies-altai-traditions-myths"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_353
X-SOURCE:IU Mathers Museum of World Cultures
DTSTART:20270130T190000Z
DTEND:20270130T200000Z
LOCATION:IU Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology\, 416 N. Indiana Ave.\
 , Bloomington\, IN 47408
GEO:39.170023;-86.526132
SUMMARY:Hidden Mythologies: Altai Traditions & Myths
DESCRIPTION:Event Details:  Now in its second year\, the Hidden Mythologi
 eslecture series explores the rich and often overlooked mythological trad
 itions of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and beyond. Through storie
 s of gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred landscapes\, and traditio
 nal beliefs\, the series examines the ways mythology reflects and preserv
 es the cultures and worldviews of diverse communities across Eurasia.  In
  this installment\, discover the mythological and spiritual traditions of
  the Altai people\, including Ülgen\, Erlik\, Ak Ana\, Mergen\, and Bai 
 Ulgan. The lecture explores shamanism and the dualism of Altai cosmogony\
 , examining the powerful beings and opposing forces that shape the Altai 
 understanding of the universe. The program concludes with a live performa
 nce of the Altai folk song Kai Kozhon.  Each program begins with a presen
 tation by Dr. Golshat Safiullina exploring an aspect of the featured cult
 ure's mythological traditions\, followed by Folk Song Club\, an interacti
 ve musical experience curated by musical director Daryana Antipova\, brin
 ging another dimension to the stories\, beliefs\, and cultural heritage e
 xplored in the lecture.  This event is held in collaboration with the Sla
 vic and East European Languages Department\, the Jacobs School of Music\,
  and the CHAKCHAK: Languages and Cultures Foundation.Parking Information:
   Limited complimentary parking is available in front of the Museum entra
 nce (https://maps.app.goo.gl/k4Txsojst2gSW5fUA) off Indiana Avenue. Free 
 street parking is available in surrounding neighborhoods on weekends and 
 weekdays after 5:00 PM.  Paid parking is also available in the University
  Collections at McCalla (https://maps.app.goo.gl/3i62KNcjDeCACruHA) parki
 ng lot off Indiana Avenue and the Indiana Memorial Union (https://maps.ap
 p.goo.gl/2n49VoLuF78o5twJA) visitor lot.  Metered street parking is avail
 able near Sample Gates and throughout downtown Bloomington\, including al
 ong Kirkwood Avenue\, East 6th Street\, East 4th Street\, North Lincoln S
 treet\, North Dunn Street\, and South Grant Street.Questions?  For access
 ibility assistance or questions about this event\, please reach out to Ca
 mden Hill at camrhill@iu.edu (mailto:camrhill@iu.edu) or (812) 855-1696.N
 ote:  By attending this event\, you agree to be in photography that may b
 e used by the University for promotional purposes. If you would not like 
 to be in any pictures\, please speak to a Museum staff member and they wi
 ll inform assigned photographers of your request. If you see that your im
 age has been used in error\, please reach out to iumaa@iu.edu (mailto:ium
 aa@iu.edu) to request that it be taken down.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Event Details:</strong></p><p>  N
 ow in its <strong>second year</strong>\, the <strong>Hidden Mythologies</
 strong><strong>lecture series</strong> explores the rich and often overlo
 oked mythological traditions of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and 
 beyond. Through stories of gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred lan
 dscapes\, and traditional beliefs\, the series examines the ways mytholog
 y reflects and preserves the cultures and worldviews of diverse communiti
 es across Eurasia.</p><p>  In this installment\, discover the mythologica
 l and spiritual traditions of the <strong>Altai people</strong>\, includi
 ng <strong>Ülgen</strong>\, <strong>Erlik</strong>\, <strong>Ak Ana</str
 ong>\, <strong>Mergen</strong>\, and <strong>Bai Ulgan</strong>. The lect
 ure explores shamanism and the dualism of Altai cosmogony\, examining the
  powerful beings and opposing forces that shape the Altai understanding o
 f the universe. The program concludes with a live performance of the Alta
 i folk song <strong><em>Kai Kozhon</em></strong><em>.</em></p><p>  Each p
 rogram begins with a presentation by <strong>Dr. Golshat Safiullina</stro
 ng> exploring an aspect of the featured culture's mythological traditions
 \, followed by Folk Song Club\, an interactive musical experience curated
  by musical director <strong>Daryana Antipova\,</strong> bringing another
  dimension to the stories\, beliefs\, and cultural heritage explored in t
 he lecture.</p><p>  This event is held in collaboration with the Slavic a
 nd East European Languages Department\, the Jacobs School of Music\, and 
 the CHAKCHAK: Languages and Cultures Foundation.</p><p><strong>Parking In
 formation:</strong></p><p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  Limited complimentary 
 parking is available in front of the <a class="external" href="https://ma
 ps.app.goo.gl/k4Txsojst2gSW5fUA">Museum entrance</a> off Indiana Avenue. 
 Free street parking is available in surrounding neighborhoods on weekends
  and weekdays after 5:00 PM.</p><p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  Paid parking 
 is also available in the <a class="external" href="https://maps.app.goo.g
 l/3i62KNcjDeCACruHA">University Collections at McCalla</a> parking lot of
 f Indiana Avenue and the <a class="external" href="https://maps.app.goo.g
 l/2n49VoLuF78o5twJA">Indiana Memorial Union</a> visitor lot.</p><p data-p
 m-slice="1 1 []">  Metered street parking is available near Sample Gates 
 and throughout downtown Bloomington\, including along Kirkwood Avenue\, E
 ast 6th Street\, East 4th Street\, North Lincoln Street\, North Dunn Stre
 et\, and South Grant Street.</p><p><strong>Questions?</strong></p><p>  Fo
 r accessibility assistance or questions about this event\, please reach o
 ut to Camden Hill at <a href="mailto:camrhill@iu.edu">camrhill@iu.edu</a>
  or (812) 855-1696.</p><p><strong>Note:</strong></p><p>  By attending thi
 s event\, you agree to be in photography that may be used by the Universi
 ty for promotional purposes. If you would not like to be in any pictures\
 , please speak to a Museum staff member and they will inform assigned pho
 tographers of your request. If you see that your image has been used in e
 rror\, please reach out to <a href="mailto:iumaa@iu.edu">iumaa@iu.edu</a>
  to request that it be taken down.</p>
